        <p>
          I co-own a general contracting company building custom homes in Tampa and Naples, so subcontractor pay applications cross my desk every month. The subs who get paid on time aren't always the biggest or the best in the field. They're the ones whose paperwork lets us approve the pay app in one pass, because our own draw to the owner or the lender depends on it.
        </p>

        <h2>Why pay apps get sent back</h2>
        <p>Almost every rejection comes from the same short list:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>The math doesn't tie.</strong> This period plus previous billing doesn't match the total, or the line items don't add up to the schedule of values.</li>
          <li><strong>Billing ahead of the work.</strong> A line is billed at 60% when the GC's walk-through says 40%. One aggressive line makes the reviewer question every other line.</li>
          <li><strong>Unapproved change orders.</strong> Extra work billed before the change order is signed gets pulled out, and sometimes the whole pay app goes back.</li>
          <li><strong>Lien waivers that don't match.</strong> Wrong form, wrong period, wrong amount, or a final waiver where a progress waiver was needed.</li>
          <li><strong>An expired certificate of insurance.</strong> Many GCs won't release payment while a sub's coverage is out of date.</li>
          <li><strong>Missing backup.</strong> Stored materials billed without invoices or photos, or the GC's required form not used.</li>
          <li><strong>Retainage calculated wrong.</strong> Retainage not held, or held at a different rate than the subcontract says.</li>
          <li><strong>Late.</strong> It arrived after the GC's cutoff, so it rolls to next month no matter how clean it is.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>The first-submission checklist</h2>
        <ol>
          <li>Use the GC's pay app form, or the format the subcontract requires. Don't send your own invoice in its place.</li>
          <li>Bill each line at the percent complete you could defend on a site walk, not what you hope to be at by month end.</li>
          <li>Check that previous billing matches what the GC approved last month, to the penny.</li>
          <li>Bill only change orders that are signed, and list each one by number and amount.</li>
          <li>Hold retainage at the rate in your subcontract and show it on the form.</li>
          <li>Attach the lien waivers the GC asks for: usually a conditional waiver for this payment and an unconditional one for the last payment you actually received.</li>
          <li>Attach a current certificate of insurance if yours renewed or expires soon.</li>
          <li>Attach backup for stored materials: invoices, photos, and where the material is stored.</li>
          <li>Send it before the GC's cutoff date, to the person and in the way the subcontract says.</li>
          <li>Keep a copy of exactly what you sent and when.</li>
        </ol>

        <h2>Where AI helps, and where it doesn't</h2>
        <p>
          AI won't fill in your pay app for you, and you shouldn't trust it with the final numbers. Where it saves real time is the checking and the writing around the pay app:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>A pre-send check.</strong> Paste your schedule of values and this period's billing and ask it to flag anything that doesn't add up, any line over 100%, and any change order you billed that isn't on your signed list. Then confirm the math in your spreadsheet. AI can make arithmetic mistakes.</li>
          <li><strong>The cover email.</strong> A short, clear submission email listing every attachment, so the reviewer doesn't have to hunt.</li>
          <li><strong>The contract checklist.</strong> Paste the payment and insurance sections of your subcontract once and get a checklist of every form, deadline and requirement you owe.</li>
          <li><strong>The follow-up.</strong> A polite, specific note when payment is late, and a firmer one if the first gets no reply.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          All four are in the free <a href="/paperwork-pack/">GC-Approved Paperwork Pack</a>, ready to copy and paste.
        </p>

        <h2>A note on Florida lien waivers</h2>
        <p>
          Florida law sets out statutory forms for lien waivers and releases, and lien rights come with strict notice deadlines under Chapter 713. Use the GC's required forms, and have your attorney review anything you're unsure about. AI is useful for organizing what you owe and when. It is not a substitute for legal advice.
        </p>

        <h2>What it's worth</h2>
        <p>
          If your pay apps routinely bounce, you're financing the job for an extra month every time. Run your own numbers in the <a href="/money-leak-calculator/">Money Leak Calculator</a>, or book a call and we'll look at a real one together.
        </p>

